>
  <div class="container section">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" style="font-size:0.9rem;color:#6b5a54;margin-bottom:16px;">
      <a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/blog/">Guides</a> &rsaquo; Chilli Heat Explained
    </nav>
    <h1>Chilli Heat Explained: SHU vs Capsaicin</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Chilli heat is measured in Scoville Heat Units (SHU) — a proxy for capsaicin concentration. But
      heat is not flavour, and it is not linear. Understanding both is what makes a specification
      reliable.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">What SHU actually measures</h2>
    <p>
      SHU quantifies the concentration of capsaicin, the compound that triggers the heat sensation on
      the palate. Sweet pepper registers under 500 SHU; the hottest common Chinese varieties — Jixin,
      Chilli King — exceed 50,000 SHU. The number is a concentration, not a "taste strength", which is
      why two chillies can share an SHU rating yet feel very different.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Heat is non-linear — dose accordingly</h2>
    <p>
      A small amount of a 50,000+ SHU variety moves a blend's heat far more than the same weight of a
      10,000 SHU variety. In practice this means extra-hot chillies are usually blended as an accent,
      and precise SHU windows — not "hot" or "medium" — are what keep a product consistent batch to
      batch.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Heat vs colour vs flavour</h2>
    <p>
      Three separate properties, controlled independently. Heat (capsaicin, SHU) is what burns; colour
      (carotenoids, ASTA) is what tints red; flavour (aroma compounds) is what you taste. A complete
      specification names all three — which is why a variety name beats a generic "chilli powder".
      See <a href="/heat-levels/high-heat/">high heat</a> and
      <a href="/specs/mesh-size/">mesh size</a> for the practical next steps.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">How SHU is measured today</h2>
    <p>
      The scale dates to 1912, when Wilbur Scoville diluted chilli extract in sugar water until a
      tasting panel could no longer detect heat — one "Scoville Heat Unit" per dilution step. Today
      the number is measured instrumentally: a laboratory extracts the chilli and quantifies its
      capsaicinoids by high-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC), then converts the concentration
      into SHU. Roughly 1 part per million of capsaicin corresponds to about 16 SHU, which is why pure
      capsaicin registers around 16 million SHU.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The capsaicinoids behind the number</h2>
    <p>
      "Capsaicin" is actually a small family of related compounds — the capsaicinoids. Capsaicin and
      dihydrocapsaicin together account for the great majority of heat in most chillies, with smaller
      amounts of nordihydrocapsaicin, homocapsaicin and homodihydrocapsaicin rounding out the mix. The
      exact ratio varies by variety, which is part of why two chillies at the same SHU can still feel
      different — the burn arrives at a different speed and lingers for a different time. This is
      another reason to specify by <em>variety and SHU window</em>, not by a single generic number.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">A practical heat guide</h2>
    <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:8px;">
      <thead>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #d8c9c0;text-align:left;">
          <th style="padding:8px;">Band</th>
          <th style="padding:8px;">SHU</th>
          <th style="padding:8px;">Typical use</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Sweet</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">&lt;500</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Colour and mild flavour — paprika</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Low heat</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">&lt;10,000</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">General seasoning, everyday blends</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Medium heat</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">10,000–20,000</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Seasoning, chilli paste, snacks</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>High heat</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">20,000–50,000</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Hot sauce, chilli oil, industrial food</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Extra hot</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">&gt;50,000</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Extreme-heat products, Sichuan-style dishes</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:8px;">
      See our <a href="/heat-levels/medium-heat/">heat level pages</a> for the variety-to-SHU mapping
      behind each band.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What is SHU and how is chilli heat measured?</h3>
      <p>SHU (Scoville Heat Units) measures chilli heat as the concentration of capsaicin — the compound that triggers the heat sensation. The higher the SHU, the hotter the chilli: sweet pepper is under 500 SHU, while extra-hot varieties exceed 50,000 SHU.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Is chilli heat linear?</h3>
      <p>No. Heat is non-linear: a small amount of a 50,000+ SHU variety moves a blend's heat far more than the same weight of a 10,000 SHU variety. That is why precise SHU windows matter in a specification.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What is the difference between heat and flavour?</h3>
      <p>Heat (capsaicin, measured in SHU) and flavour (aroma compounds) are separate and vary independently. Two chillies can share the same SHU but taste completely different — another reason to name the variety, not just the heat.</p>
    </div>

